1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,670
Loopback Transformer.

2
00:00:04,590 --> 00:00:08,700
A looped Transformer runs the
same blocks again and again.

3
00:00:09,230 --> 00:00:10,700
That saves parameters,

4
00:00:11,130 --> 00:00:12,460
but with plain addition,

5
00:00:12,670 --> 00:00:15,400
the updates from every pass just pile up.

6
00:00:16,700 --> 00:00:18,450
So we built Loopback.

7
00:00:19,100 --> 00:00:23,330
It stores each block's update and
keeps the input on its own path.

8
00:00:24,100 --> 00:00:26,990
A decay limits how much old updates count,

9
00:00:27,380 --> 00:00:29,730
and attention picks which ones to read.

10
00:00:31,310 --> 00:00:35,580
Here a standard Transformer and
Loopback read the same new text.

11
00:00:36,390 --> 00:00:38,430
Loopback is 29% smaller,

12
00:00:38,430 --> 00:00:40,320
and its loss still ends lower.

13
00:00:42,890 --> 00:00:46,220
From 191M to 3B parameters,

14
00:00:46,730 --> 00:00:49,640
Loopback gets lower loss than the standard

15
00:00:49,640 --> 00:00:52,700
Transformer with 40–75% of its parameters.

16
00:00:54,980 --> 00:00:57,230
Against seven other looped designs,

17
00:00:57,600 --> 00:01:00,032
it has the lowest loss and the highest

18
00:01:00,032 --> 00:01:03,190
benchmark average at all
three sizes we tested.

19
00:01:05,560 --> 00:01:07,200
And at 3B parameters,

20
00:01:07,200 --> 00:01:10,130
it more than doubles
word-problem accuracy,

21
00:01:10,560 --> 00:01:13,510
from 2.6% to 6.4%.

22
00:01:16,150 --> 00:01:17,280
That's Loopback.

23
00:01:17,670 --> 00:01:18,460
Fewer parameters,

24
00:01:18,990 --> 00:01:19,520
lower loss.

25
00:01:20,550 --> 00:01:22,660
The paper and code are
on our project page.

